The <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-duo/">iPhone Duo</a> will work with the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/apple-pencil/">Apple Pencil</a>, but support is limited. Apple says that you need the &zwnj;Apple Pencil&zwnj; with USB-C for the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj;, so if you have the &zwnj;Apple Pencil&zwnj; Pro, you may be out of luck.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2023/10/Apple-Pencil-USB-C-sliding-cap-feature.jpg" alt="" width="1960" height="1102" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-917176" /><br/>
It's likely the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; doesn't support the &zwnj;Apple Pencil&zwnj; Pro because there's no magnetic surface for charging the pencil. The &zwnj;Apple Pencil&zwnj; Pro charges magnetically when connected to a compatible <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad/">iPad</a>, while the &zwnj;Apple Pencil&zwnj; with USB-C charges with a USB-C port hidden behind a sliding cap.<br/>
<br/>
The &zwnj;Apple Pencil&zwnj; with USB-C does not support pressure sensitivity, double-tap, barrel roll, haptic feedback, squeeze, or <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/find-my/">Find My</a>, but it does have tilt sensitivity. Apple charges &#36;79 for the &zwnj;Apple Pencil&zwnj; with USB-C and &#36;129 for the &zwnj;Apple Pencil&zwnj; Pro.<br/>
<br/>
Apple says that an &zwnj;Apple Pencil&zwnj; will work with both the inner and outer &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; displays. It's the first iPhone that works with &zwnj;Apple Pencil&zwnj;.<br/>
<br/>
&zwnj;Apple Pencil&zwnj; support won't be available right when the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; launches, and Apple says it's coming later this year.<br/>
